# Fail2ban — Ubuntu 24.04 LTS

Target platform: **Ubuntu 24.04 LTS only.**

Mission: PBX server (Asterisk + API on 44300). Jails protect SSH (admin), Asterisk (SIP/registration), repeat offenders, and API probes.

## Installer behaviour

- **Does not** symlink `jail.local` (Ubuntu 24.04 fail2ban breaks on a full custom `jail.local` with `%(auth_log)s`).
- Symlinks `jail.d/pbx3-jails.conf` and `jail.d/pbx3-api.conf` → `/etc/fail2ban/jail.d/`
- Symlinks `action.d/shorewall.local` → `/etc/fail2ban/action.d/shorewall.local`
- Removes a legacy pbx3 `jail.local` symlink if present
- Runs `fail2ban-client -t` then reload when config is valid

## Jails

| Jail | Config file | Purpose |
|------|-------------|---------|
| **sshd** | `jail.d/pbx3-jails.conf` | SSH brute-force. 3 failures in 10 min → ban 24 h. Log: `/var/log/auth.log`. |
| **asterisk** | `jail.d/pbx3-jails.conf` | SIP/PJSIP auth failures. 5 in 10 min → ban 10 min. Log: `/var/log/asterisk/messages`. |
| **recidive** | `jail.d/pbx3-jails.conf` | Repeat offenders across jails. 3 bans in 24 h → ban 1 week. |
| **nginx-badbots** | `jail.d/pbx3-api.conf` | Bad User-Agents on API nginx log. 1 match in 24 h → ban 24 h. |

## Ubuntu 24.04 notes

- Fail2ban 1.0.2-3 can fail under Python 3.12 (`No module named 'asynchat'`). Use fail2ban **1.1.0 or later**.
- Validate config: `sudo fail2ban-client -t`
- Status: `sudo fail2ban-client status`
- Test filters:  
  `fail2ban-regex /var/log/auth.log /etc/fail2ban/filter.d/sshd.conf`  
  `fail2ban-regex /var/log/asterisk/messages /etc/fail2ban/filter.d/asterisk.conf`  
  `fail2ban-regex /var/log/nginx/access.log /etc/fail2ban/filter.d/nginx-badbots.conf`

## Shipped files

| File | Purpose |
|------|--------|
| `jail.d/pbx3-jails.conf` | sshd, asterisk, recidive + `[DEFAULT]` banaction/ignoreip |
| `jail.d/pbx3-api.conf` | nginx-badbots for pbx3api |
| `action.d/shorewall.local` | Shorewall ban action |
